the gate's own wiring.


== 7. The blanker, because it cost 3 minutes of every CI run

Reading a `cfg` correctly requires ignoring comments. The first version blanked them character by
character in Python and the full-tree scan took long enough that the guard was the slowest step in
its job. The replacement jumps between interesting positions with one compiled pattern:

    NOISE = re.compile(r"/[/*]|r#*\"|['\"]")
    jump = NOISE.search(text, i)

Since a blanker that is subtly wrong silently changes every row, the equivalence was proven rather
than eyeballed. `scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py` exposes `blank_noise(raw, keep_strings=..)`;
the same file's per-character predecessor was run against it on 400 real `.rs` files from the tree,
in both modes, and then against a whole-tree scan:

    blanker equivalence: 800 file x mode comparisons, no difference
    full-tree scan: fast 8.5s, per-character 18.2s, 2.1x, identical rows: True (13011 rows)

13,011 is what `scan()` returns before filtering; `ledger_rows()` keeps the 1,108 that some
platform does not build. The comparison of the two scans is of every column of every row, so the
optimisation is the same function measured twice, not a new one.

The `keep_strings=True` pass is built lazily, only for rows that are actually platform-gated,
because the assumptions column is the only consumer that needs string contents.


== 8. Wiring

`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, immediately after the spawn-cwd portability pair:

    # Platform-gated tests: which tests some OS does not even build, and which of those gates
    # nothing platform-specific. The ledger refuses new gating without a reason and refuses the
    # blind-file and assumption-free counts going up.
    python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py
    python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet \
      --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv \
      --max-unreviewed 1108 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 \
      --max-assumption-free 443

The two budgets in that block are the values as imported; section 10 records the pair they were
lowered to on 2026-10-04 and shows the lowering being checked.

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` carries the same two commands as one entry in its `gates=()` array, so
the local Docker entry and CI run the same guard with the same budgets. `check-guard-wiring.py`
enforces both directions of that mirror -- that a script in `scripts/ci/` is reachable, and that a
reachable one is either run by the Docker entry or recorded in `scripts/ci/docker-entry-ci-only.tsv`:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (50 files in scripts/ci/, 49 reachable, 45 run by
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)


== 9. What this does not claim

  * `cfg` expressions are read as written on the item. `cfg_attr` is understood only in the
    `cfg_attr(<platform>, ignore = "...")` shape; any other `cfg_attr` is invisible here.
  * `cfg` applied from inside a macro (`include!`, a custom test-attribute macro) is invisible.
  * a file pulled in by a platform-gated `mod` declaration does not inherit that gate, so a file
    compiled only on unix reads as ungated. The scan covers every `.rs` file except `target/`,
    `.git/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/` and `test-results/`, including members outside `crates/`, so
    nothing cargo builds is graded by a narrower walk.
  * `cfg(not(unix))` counts as covering `other` as well as `windows`, because that is what it
    covers. Nothing here is evidence that a platform leg ran anything: the ledger records what the
    source excludes, which is a different fact from what a runner executed.
  * a `none` in `assumptions` is a claim about the test's own text, wrong in the two ways spelled
    out in section 4.
  * the 1,108 rows carry the import marker, so this first commit records the debt without pretending
    each gate was reviewed. Reviewing one means replacing its reason and lowering `--max-unreviewed`.
    Section 10 records the first two rows coming off and the first budget going down.


== 10. 2026-10-04: two gates actually paid off, and the two budgets that can only fall went down

The budgets in this file are ratchets, so the first honest test of that claim is whether a later
batch can lower them. The 2026-10-04 Windows batch lowered two of them, and it did so by removing
gating rather than by editing rows.

Which two rows left, by comparing the ledger against `HEAD` on row identity (`kind`, `file`,
`function`, `runs_on`, `extra_cfg`, `assumptions`), so line-number churn above each row does not
show up as a change:

    $ git show HEAD:scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv > pgt-head.tsv
    $ cut -f1,3,5,6,7,8 pgt-head.tsv | tail -n +2 | sort > head.key
    $ cut -f1,3,5,6,7,8 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv | tail -n +2 | sort > now.key
    $ comm -23 head.key now.key              # rows in HEAD that are gone
    cfg-gate  .../lsp/tests/mock_servers.rs  a_candidate_that_cannot_even_be_spawned_is_skipped
              linux+macos+other-unix  none
    cfg-gate  .../lsp/tests/mock_servers.rs  a_stub_that_refuses_to_run_is_not_an_interpreter
              linux+macos+other-unix  none
    $ comm -13 head.key now.key              # rows that appeared
    (nothing)

Both were `#[cfg(unix)]` tests guarding the interpreter probe, and both were `assumptions=none`,
which is why removing them moved the named count by the same two. They are the worst kind of row in
this ledger: the failure they guard is the Microsoft Store execution alias, which exists only on
Windows, and the gate is exactly what kept Windows from ever running them. Un-gating them is the
point of the whole inventory, not a number going down for its own sake.

The blocker was the fixture, not the assertion. Building a candidate that "spawns, complains, and
leaves" meant writing a shell script with a `#!` line and `chmod 755`-ing it, which is not something
Windows can spawn. The stub is now built out of the interpreter the host has already proved works:
Python takes the first `-c` and treats everything after it as `sys.argv`, so

    python -c "<stub body>" -u -c "<the probe's own script>"

runs the stub body and never reaches the probe's script, which is precisely the alias's shape. That
is portable, so the tests no longer need a platform. `docs/verification/windows-test-failures-2026-10-03.log`
section 2 carries the fixture detail, including why the stub's message had to move into a file.

Measured after, same command as section 3:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py
    platform-gated tests: 1080 in 249 files
    platform-conditional #[ignore] (exists there, skipped there): 26
    gated and also #[ignore]d everywhere: 0
    gated with no platform assumption in the body: 441 in 99 files
    would compile on:
      linux       988 of 1080
      macos       850 of 1080
      other-unix  809 of 1080
      windows     62 of 1080
      other       29 of 1080
    files with gated tests and no test that runs on the platform:
      windows     74
      macos       11
      linux       10

The ledger file is 1,107 lines, 1,106 rows under the header: 1,080 gates plus the 26
platform-conditional `#[ignore]`s. `mock_servers.rs` is no longer one of the blind files, which is
why the file count fell 250 to 249 while `windows` blind files stayed at 74 -- that file had no
Windows-running test before and has no gated test now, so it left one column and never was in the
other. Windows coverage is 62 of 1,080 rather than 62 of 1,082: two tests moved from "not built
here" to "built and run here", which does not change the numerator.

Both budgets were lowered at both wiring points, and the lowering is checked in both directions:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet \
        --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv \
        --max-unreviewed 1106 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 \
        --max-assumption-free 441
    (rc=0)
    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet \
        --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv \
        --max-unreviewed 1105 --max-blind-windows 74 --max-blind-macos 11 \
        --max-assumption-free 441
    FAIL: 1106 rows still carry the import marker, over the budget of 1105
    (rc=1)

So section 8's command line is superseded by the one above; the budgets that were 1108 and 443 are
now 1106 and 441, and the next batch that un-gates something has to lower them again.

Lowering them meant editing two files. The third file that had them written in it was not edited,
and that is what section 11 is about.

== 11. 2026-10-04, later: the lowering broke the gate, and the thing that was stale was the test

The full local sweep over the tree of section 10 ran every guard in
`scripts/verify-gates.sh`. One gate failed, and it was this one:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh
    ...
    FAIL: test_the_shipped_ledger_carries_unreviewed_rows_for_the_budget_to_mean_anything
              (__main__.LiveRepository)
        AssertionError: 1106 != 1108

    FAIL: test_the_shipped_ledger_names_gates_with_nothing_to_gate
              (__main__.LiveRepository)
        AssertionError: 441 != 443

    Ran 35 tests in 11.307s
    FAILED (failures=2)
    ...
    FAILED gates: platform-gated tests
    26 gate(s) run, 4 skipped, 1 failed

Nothing was wrong with the ledger or with the guard. The two numbers were written down in three
places -- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, and a pair of module constants
at the top of `scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py` -- and section 10 had updated the first two.
So the case whose stated job is "the shipped ledger is exactly at the cap CI enforces" was
comparing the ledger against its own stale copy and reporting the ledger as the thing that had
moved. The gate was right to fail the sweep: a cap written in three places had just silently
diverged in two of them.

The fix removes the third copy rather than restating it. `call_site` reads the gate's own command
out of a call site (anchored on the invocation, backslash continuations joined) and `wired_budgets`
pulls the four `--max-*` values from it; the live-repository cases run at whatever the wiring says.

    scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py:82-118   call_site / wired_budgets / BUDGETS

Two live-repository cases came out of the same incident. The first is the one that had been missing
in another direction: ci.yml and verify-in-docker.sh each pass the four budgets, and
`check-guard-wiring.py` only proves both name the gate, never that they say the same numbers, so
the leg nobody runs locally could have been enforcing a different cap from the leg that blocks
merges.

    def test_the_two_call_sites_pass_the_same_four_numbers(self) -> None:
        first, second = (BUDGETS[rel] for rel in CALL_SITES)
        self.assertEqual(first, second, {rel: BUDGETS[rel] for rel in CALL_SITES})

The second asks whether the caps still bite at all, because a cap that has drifted far above the
debt passes every check above and polices nothing. One run, all four caps lowered together; the
guard reports every budget it exceeded, so this one run covers all four numbers:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet \
        --check-baseline scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv \
        --max-unreviewed 1105 --max-blind-windows 73 --max-blind-macos 10 \
        --max-assumption-free 440
    FAIL: 1106 rows still carry the import marker, over the budget of 1105
    FAIL: 74 files have platform-gated tests and nothing that runs on Windows, over the budget of 73
    FAIL: 11 files have platform-gated tests and nothing that runs on macOS, over the budget of 10
    FAIL: 441 gated tests show no platform assumption in their body, over the budget of 440; ...
    (rc=1)

Four fixtures cover the reader itself, because "read the number from the command" has the same
shape as every other claim here: a reader that searched the file instead of the command would find
a comment quoting an older budget and assert a number nothing enforces. A prose mention of all four
flags above the real command must not be what gets parsed; a later step's `--max-unreviewed 9` in
the same job must not be either; a file that never runs the guard is an error; a call site missing
one of the four flags is an error naming the flag.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py
    Ran 41 tests in 28.420s

    OK

Six mutations against the shipped files, each restored byte-identically and cmp-verified:

